// IRIW with an address dependency between each reader's two loads.
//
// rmm-zoo property: mca (Atomicity / Multicopy atomic).
// Source: Sarkar et al., "Understanding POWER Multiprocessors" (PLDI 2011);
//         Pulte et al., "Simplifying ARM Concurrency" (POPL 2018);
//         Alglave et al., "Herding Cats" (TOPLAS 2014).
//
// This is THE canonical multicopy-atomicity discriminator. The address
// dependency (`rp + (r1 - r1)`, the C-level analogue of ARM's `EOR r2,r0,r0;
// LDR r4,[r3,r2]`) removes load-load reordering as an explanation, so the
// outcome can only arise if the write to x reaches reader 1 before it reaches
// reader 2 -- i.e. only if writes are NOT multicopy atomic.
//
// Reference verdicts: POWER and ARMv7 (non-MCA) ALLOW.
//                     ARMv8, RVWMO, x86-TSO and SC (MCA) FORBID.
//
// Note for semantic-dependency models (MRD / sMRD): the dependency here is
// syntactic but semantically dead -- `r1 - r1` is 0 whatever r1 is -- so a model
// that computes dependencies semantically drops it and behaves as for
// IRIW+rlx.lit. That divergence is the point of keeping both files.
x := 0;
y := 0;
{
  x := 1
} ||| {
  y := 1
} ||| {
  r1 := x;
  rp := &y;
  r2 := *(rp + (r1 - r1))
} ||| {
  r3 := y;
  rq := &x;
  r4 := *(rq + (r3 - r3))
}
%%
allow (r1 = 1 && r2 = 0 && r3 = 1 && r4 = 0) [Power]
